A||N||23||bHooper||any one ever make a major career change,or go back to school?||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||04-19-2001||09:28 AM||||?<P>i'm in my mid 30's, and have spent the last 8 years in the textile industry... a industry that govt. and banking have decided is dead, domestically.<P>either way, i am making a career change... leaving all that behind me somehow.  i had thought that i wanted to get into pharmaceutical sales, or some other mfging sales.  <P>however, i went to a career counselor yesterday, and took some test's, and they all pointed towards computers (the same thing that my fiance' and friends have all suggested)... hmm wonder what i should do?, or other hands on detailed profesions (such as engineer, biologist and even ::cringe:: school teacher.  some sort of programing, or other thing... like IT, web design, etc. <P>so, my question, has anyoine here ever drastically changed their career's in mid life?  how dificult ws it?  how did you financially survive going back to school? <P>fortunately for me, it appears that i may qualify for a Pell Grant (i am in the application process), and other benifits given to "displaced workers".  however, the unemployment insurance part will barely cover my fixed expenses.  so obviousely, there is a lot to consider b4 i do this; i have a housepayment to make, a leased car (thats due in september, so that's not so bad), 2 dogs and am getting married in June.<P>thanks for listening!  i consider a lot of you guys to be good friends, full of good advise (at times   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>hoop<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's<BR>and spellcheck crash's windows<BR>Poly Bushings<BR>Accel Coil<p>[This message has been edited by bHooper (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jaygee79||04-19-2001||09:45 AM||||You'll have to hold down a job while you're in school.  My cousin is going through this right now, and I'm a student.  Don't believe everything those career tests tell you.  I took one and it said that I should be a Roman Catholic Priest (I'm not Catholic and I'm a female).  There are lots of loans you can get.  You'll have to pay them all back though.  If you qualify for financial aid you can get that too.  If you are lucky you can receive more than you need and get the rest in a refund check.  You have to pay this back too, but it helps when you have no money.<P>Hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jaime<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooheey1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||04-19-2001||09:55 AM||||It's a scarey thing, but can be very rewarding. Take the time to decide where your passion lies. "do what you love, and the money will follow"<BR>I closed down a contracting business after about ten years of operation. I got into technical writing and have enjoyed the freedom. I realize it's not what I really want to do, but it pays well, and leaves me lots of free time. Good luck whatever you choose to do.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000003||deceler8||04-19-2001||09:55 AM||||Best way to do this is find a company that assists with tuition...this is how I got my advanced degrees without getting into debt.<P>Funny thing is I got canned almost as soon as the ink was dry on my thesis...been constructively unemployed ever since.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wade87GT||04-19-2001||10:02 AM||||bHooper, I'm doing exactly that right now. Two years ago, my wife (at the time) convinced me to retire early from the military so I could go to school full time. Complete career change at 41 yrs of age. She left right after I retired and took her great income (and her new implants) with her. That left me, unemployed with all the bills, a modest retirement check (she gets 28% of it), two little dogs (who kept me out of the deep end), and big legal expenses for the divorce. She also busted the nose of my Fiero, which I still haven't been able to fix yet. Fortunately, I have the G.I. bill to help pay for school and a part time job at the college as a computer tech and network administrator. It hasn't been easy but I'm now two classes away from my A.S. in Computer Information Science. Even though this field is going through its low point, I'm not too concerned. Too bad I'm not into country music, I could write a song and probably retire now. Anyway, go for all the school grants that you can. Make that your part time job, researching, filling out the forms, and submitting to them all. There's alot of grant money that doesn't get awarded because they didn't receive enough applicants. Check out this page for starters:<BR> <A HREF="http://websearch.cs.com/gateway/results/gateway-frameset.adp?hit_url=http%3a%2f%2fwww%2efedmoney%2eorg&type=I&first=31&dfirst=1&sTerm=government%20grants&type=I&brand=GWY&uType=&gf" TARGET=_blank>http://websearch.cs.com/gateway/results/gateway-frameset.adp?hit_url=http%3a%2f%2fwww%2efedmoney%2eorg&type=I&first=31&dfirst=1&sTerm=government%20grants&type=I&brand=GWY&uType=&gf</A>  irst=10<P>Sometimes in life, you have to do something for YOURSELF.<P>Wade<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000005||Songman||04-19-2001||10:09 AM||||Not exactly the same as what you are doing, but I also spent 8 years in the textile industry and then decided to go follow my dreams...<P>I was Corporate Publisher for Shaw Industries for 8 years. Being in the industry, you know Shaw. It was was a great career! But I wasn't happy...<P>So one day I walked in and handed in a two week notice and moved to Nashville to be a country music singer like I had always dreamed of...<P>Lots of people thought I was crazy, but lots also respected me for having the balls to try it. I have never once regretted it! I love my life and it keeps getting better everyday...<P>My advice is always - Go For It! I wish you luck....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||04-19-2001||12:03 PM||||<B>WOW!</B> such a big response, so quickly!<P>Wade, thanks for the web site.  That has a lot of usefull information!  Have you used some of these grants to suppliment your income?<P>One of the things that i have investigated it the WIA (Workforce Investment ACt), which helps to reeducate displaced workers, while extending their unemployment benefits (thats almost a laugh) for up to two years, while you go back to school!<P>It's funny, but I never thought I would be in this position... but then I guess no one ever does until they are.<P>Thanks for the info, guys!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000007||Richard Parnell||04-19-2001||03:00 PM||||This is long. I'm 54 and am in the process of making a big career/lifestyle change.  My wife and (I thought friend) of 32 years decided to have an affair and then when I confronted her she said she wanted a divorce.  That came about after her mother died last year and she (my wife) inherited close to a million bucks.  First thing that gave me an indication that there was a problem was when she came back from burying her mother was within 15 minutes of getting home she told me she had inherited this money and that she had put it into a trust where I couldn't touch it. You could have knocked me over with a feather.  I would never have even thought about doing that sort of thing.  I would have said upon returning home, "honey, we just inherited a bunch of money.  Let's take a vacation and travel for a while." Then came the affair and ensuing divorce over the next nightmare 10 months.  I realized that all the blame was not hers in this.  I had not been paying enough attention to her and was sometimes not a lot of fun to be around and I drank too much.  Why?  Because I was working at a career that I was not happy with.  I work in telecommunications.  Some people may think that's great and for them it may be.  For me it was boring.  I have a knack for that sort of thing but it was boring.  I did it because the money was good and it was easy for me.  Well now I'm divorced, having to sell my house at Sunset Beach here on Oahu.  I'm still working for now, as an analyst for the Navy until I can get my life in order.  What do I plan on doing?  A number of things.  I want to get back into painting and sculpting, just art in general.  I also want to market some ideas.  I want to write.  I want to do a bunch of things and just have some money coming in from different sources.  If one takes off great.  But the main point is I want to do things that interest me.  I have no responsibilities.  My sons are grown (20 & 22) all I have to do for them is be there when and if they need me.  But I want to start living my life for me not someone else.  You may think you know someone but you never really do. Love someone, yes, but don't let them be everything for you. So have fun, life is short.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jake_Dragon||04-19-2001||06:06 PM||||I was a Navy welder and worked as a civilian welding for 15 years. 4 years ago I went to a business school for computer repair and networking, it took 2 years to complete the course going 3 nights a week. I am now one of 2 network annalist at a large clinic in charge of over 20 NT servers, 14 Novell servers and 4 Unix systems.<BR>Don't be afraid if you want it anything is attainable you just have to work at it.<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by Jake_Dragon (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000009||hugh||04-19-2001||09:21 PM||||I had a long response for you and lost it.But the bottom line was I did it and I'm glad I did.If you don't try it you will never know.It"s better to say "I tried"than "What if"||||reg||1
Z||000010||MrPBody||04-19-2001||09:44 PM||||In 1982, after a dozen years as a mechanic and parts manager, I went for a Mechanical Design degree.  (I figured that <I>I</I> could design better stuff than some of the cars I worked on!)  Working and going to school was a <I>***** </I>, but that was my only option -- nobody else to pay my way.<P>The Mechanical Design course included basic -- and BASIC -- computer operation and programming, and computer-aided design (CAD).  As a result, I have since worked as a CAD drafter, systems manager, and now a data analyst.  I have not worked in Mechanical Design, but the knowledge has been helpful in keeping my strange old cars alive.<P>So what's my point?  Your career may not go in the direction you intended, but it will go somewhere!<P>Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||04-19-2001||10:38 PM||||Gee, I've had several "careers".   I went to technical college after high school to study HVAC repair and worked in that for a few years, but decided I didn't want to make my living crawling under people's houses.  I had a part time job at a movie theater then, so I applied for and started managing movie theaters.  Fun job.  I really liked, but it didn't pay enough, so at the ripe old age of 25, I headed to college to study Mechanical Engineering at NCSU.  Got a great job with NASA at Kennedy Space Center, and then Bill Clinton got elected.  Goodbye NASA.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  After that I drifted from crap job to crap job till I got on as a temp at IBM in their computer production at RTP.  I'd always been a computer geek, I just never figured computers would be my career.  That was 4 years ago, and I'm now working as a Technical Analyst (not for IBM), and I'm an Accredited Compaq Technicain (among other certs).  I'll be going for my MCSE 2000 later this year and my Cisco certification early this summer.  So, I'm not at all where I figured I'd be by the time I was in my mid-30's, but I'm doing better now than I ever have and I'm quite happy with my job.  Still, nothing will ever compare to NASA - that took a long time to let go of, but it's still a good job.  Hell, maybe one day I'll try to get back on with NASA as a computer geek instead of an engineer.  Bottom line, when it's time to make a change, go for it.  You can start over from scratch many times in life.  I have, and I've come out of it better than before.  Better, stronger, faster.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  ||||reg||6
Z||000012||BENGAL4||04-19-2001||11:41 PM||||double post<p>[This message has been edited by BENGAL4 (edited 04-19-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000013||BENGAL4||04-19-2001||11:43 PM||||I was 30 when I went from a machine operator to an inside salesman. It was a company that makes solder and brazing alloys. While I was there, I learned all I could about why they worked and how. To make a long story short, the change led me to what I do today. It is in the welding and construction supply business. I love it and it is profitable. <P>Hoop, make the change. It you do not take some chances in your life, it can get very boring. Good luck.  <P>Bill<P>||||reg||10
Z||000014||AkursedX||04-20-2001||02:10 AM||||Man, hearing some of these stories can scare alot of us youngsters!  The closest I can relate to a major change would be that I stopped playing football at my college, and started a pretty good coaching career at my old high school coaching football track and field and powerlifting, then I decided coaching was not my thing and I decided running was.  It's the same idea on a smaller scales.  Who knows where life will take you.<P>I'm still in school earning my Criminal Justice Degree, and I have a pretty good idea that I am not going to head into the Criminal Justice field, I just have no passion for it anymore.  So I'm looking at my future with an open mind, an hopefully I'll find something halfway decent to do.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||85Josh||04-20-2001||02:39 PM||||Well, I just got laid off today, with no notice, so I might be heading for a new career myself. I guess if all else fails, I can always go active-duty Army, the National Guard just isn't enough to make unemployment viable.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||04-22-2001||10:23 AM||||I'm 33 and made a huge career change about 18 months ago.  I drove a truck for almost 15 years and got burned out.  Now I manage a convenience store.  I don't know if my particular choice was a good one, but there's nothing wrong with a career change if you are unsatisfied with the career you now have.  BTW--If you "cringe" at the thought of becoming a teacher, DON'T DO IT!!!  It takes a lot to teach.  The pay usually isn't very good and sometimes the working conditions are less than desirable.  Don't think that it would be a great job because you only have to work 9 months a year.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000017||bHooper||04-22-2001||10:46 AM||||there are many reasons that i "cringe' about being a teacher. <P>1) the money... the money... sucks!<BR>2) my mother has been an teachers assistant for almost 20 years.<BR>3) my fiance' is a elementary school counselor.<BR>4) i don't like kids.<BR>5) i don't like kids.<P>i am reallly leaning towards computers, and information tech.  there is a $ there, and a future.  not to mention the posibility of having my own independance again, one day!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000018||Cameron||04-23-2001||02:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>i am reallly leaning towards computers, and information tech.  there is a $ there, and a future.  not to mention the posibility of having my own independance again, one day!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ok your logic is not entirely correct.  I work in the computer industry right now and it is IMPOSSIBLE to find a good job.  Ya there is money there but it is impossible to get a hold of.  I am starting to think about getting into car racing.  I don't have a good name so it'd be tough, but is do able.  If you do go into the computer industry however make sure you have a degree in Computer Science, or you'll have the same problems as me right now.<P>Oh well<BR><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||bHooper||04-26-2001||03:32 PM||||i met with the counselor today, and it appears that i will qualify for a local program to teach people in IT!  they offer several different certifications.<P>i may also be lucky enough to have them pick up some of the expense in going to community college.  i am also going to actively seeek funding to pick up funding to go after a second batchelors degree, or a masters, in IT!<P>as for the Job search, i had a nice interview with a pharmaceutical company, yesterday, at a job fair.  i will still persue this, until i am able to get ceritfied.<P>buy, i tell you... not haveing a job is a full time business!  in two weeks, i have only had one day to do nothing... and i thought i was actually going to get my house painted through all of this!<P>::fingers crossed::<P>hoop||||reg||7
Z||000020||Wade87GT||04-26-2001||07:40 PM||||hoop, since you already have a BS (THAT'S what gets you to the interview) you might consider getting one of those IT certifications. Preferably one closest to your interests/talents (MOUS, MCSE, CCNA, etc.). Or maybe a AS in Computer Information Technology. Check with the local schools/colleges and see if they have any openings for a computer/network lab tech. That way you get coursework, experience and some networking (interpersonal) all at the same time. That's what I'm doing right now and it's a good deal for me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000021||bHooper||04-26-2001||08:01 PM||||my BS is in Appropriate Technology (look that one up on the net!)... not related to computers at all!||||reg||1
Z||000022||Wade87GT||04-27-2001||09:48 AM||||In the Information Tech field, it doesn't really matter what your BS is in (unless you're going for programming). The fact that you have a degree is the plus. The people getting the jobs are the ones who can do it now, meaning talent and ability. Good luck<P>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000023||turbo86se||04-28-2001||02:26 PM||||Except for my job detailing cars at Saturn many years ago, I've always worked in the food service industry. I've worked at the same restaurant bartending for the past few years and I do enjoy it. However, I know restaurant management is not for me, I've tried it. So last year I returned to school and am pursuing a Criminal Justice Associates degree. I've got this summer and fall semester to go, and then I'll graduate and hopefully start working for the County police.<BR>It's going to be an interesting transition. Culture shock maybe.<BR>I'll go from saying,"I'm sorry your steak wasn't cooked right", or something like that, to "shut the **** up or I'll haul your a** in!"  LOL   j/k<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=1676320&p=20236791"> ||||reg||1
